Grand Theft Auto VI is out here breaking every record: the game generated $3.79 billion in "day one retail sales," Rockstar Games said on Friday. That's a whole lot of love &#x2014; it's like Jason- and Lucia-levels of love, actually (if you play the game correctly).

Rockstar is using its own internal estimate through the end of Thursday. The eye-popping number includes pre-sales and makes GTA VI the "highest-grossing entertainment property" of all time, the Take-Two label announced.

It's a "new record for entertainment across any medium" and "the biggest entertainment launch in history," per Rockstar. GTA VI sold 32.5 million units pre-release. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;It is with immense gratitude that we enter a new chapter of Rockstar Games with the release of Grand Theft Auto VI ,&#x201D; said Sam Houser, president and founder of Rockstar. &#x201C;We are incredibly humbled by the response from players around the world and we couldn&#x2019;t be more excited to share this moment with you.&#x201D;

For the 10 of you who have not yet bought it, Grand Theft Auto VI is available at the PlayStation Store, Microsoft Store, Rockstar Games Store, and global retailers and storefronts. A PC launch will come at some point, as will GTA VI Online .

For costume designers Janie Bryant and John Glaser, outfitting a character is another form of storytelling.

&#x201C;When an actor appears on screen, you see the costume before they even say a word," says Bryant, an Emmy winner whose credits include "Mad Men," "Deadwood" and Taylor Sheridan shows including "Dutton Ranch" and other "Yellowstone" spinoffs. "That&#x2019;s the magic of costume design." Related Stories 'Chasing Summer' Director Josephine Decker Says That ‘Filmmaking Is Lonelier Than Ever,’ So It’s Time to Build a Community Tony Award Winner Lois Smith Joins Colin West’s ‘Outside of Cleveland’: ‘She’s an Icon’ (EXCLUSIVE)

Bryant and Glaser, an Emmy winner who has worked on "Bridgerton," "Gotham" and "Harry Potter 20th Anniversary: Return to Hogwarts," joined Variety&#x2019;s senior film reporter Rebecca Rubin in partnership with the Round Top Film Festival for a panel titled, "Who, What, Wear: The Importance of Costume in Storytelling." blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

For both, their work starts with the script.

&#x201C;It's kind of our bible,&#x201D; Bryant said. &#x201C;We can see the period. We can read how these characters are speaking to one another, the mood, the feeling. The script is everything.&#x201D;

Adds Glaser, &#x201C;The script is our bible, but you have to dissect it.&#x201D;

Once they have a sense of the characters, they can start to build out their looks. That involves extensive research, especially if they are curating a wardrobe from decades (or centuries) ago, rather than a modern story. In some instances, they are laboring over individually tailored items, dyeing pieces to achieve the perfect shade or sourcing the perfect item. Each costume requires multiple fittings on the actor, as well as creating duplicates for stunt doubles.

&#x201C;Doing a contemporary show is a little different from doing a period show,&#x201D; Bryant said. &#x201C;[A period piece] is more collaborative with actors because most of the actors don&#x2019;t know the period very well, so you are guiding them in the direction you want them to go.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Glaser was a little more blunt, noting that costume designers have more control with period pieces.

&#x201C;[Actors] can&#x2019;t have an opinion because they don&#x2019;t know what they&#x2019;re talking about, which makes a big difference,&#x201D; Glaser says. &#x201C;Modern clothes, everybody has an opinion about everything. &#x2018;I saw this skirt at this store and blah blah blah.&#x2019;"

The costume designers have mixed feelings about incorporating actors&#x2019; input into their looks. Bryant is open to collaboration, especially when working on movies. &#x201C;Film is a collaborative art, and everybody wants to feel included," she said. "I feel like I am a good politician because I do like to hear them and then have another suggestion,&#x201D; Bryant said.

Glaser has more reservations about feedback, likening a costume fitting to therapy.

&#x201C;You have to find out who you&#x2019;re talking to: the person, the actor or the character,&#x201D; Glaser says. &#x201C;I always listen if it&#x2019;s coming from a place that they want to make their character better. But if it&#x2019;s coming from their insecurities, you have to become the therapist.&#x201D;

That aspect of the job — serving as a support system for actors — is one that Glaser and Bryant say they take seriously.

&#x201C;What&#x2019;s very important is that you gain the confidence of the actor in the fitting room," Glaser says. "You have to be there when that costume is first seen to give them that support and confidence.&#x201D;

Bryant adds, &#x201C;When the actors come to see us, we're the first person that they see. A lot of times they're really scared and they don't want to be that vulnerable, so I think having that understanding and compassion is so important.&#x201D;

Bowen Yang has issued an apology following backlash for using an antisemitic slur, reportedly coined by the KKK, to describe Jewish singer Regina Spektor in a resurfaced episode of the Las Culturistas podcast .

&#x201C;I want to genuinely apologize for using a word on the podcast without knowing its antisemitic history, especially since we&#x2019;ve repudiated antisemitism on the show as recently as the last two weeks,&#x201D; Yang posted on Instagram on Friday.

&#x201C;Particularly upsetting to realize that I adopted language popularized by far-right movements whose rhetoric we should be wary of and act against,&#x201D; Yang added. In the offending podcast, the veteran comic called Spektor a &#x201C;Zio&#x201C; (short for &#x201C;Zionist&#x201C;), a pejorative term said to have been first used by former KKK Grand Wizard David Duke and then as an antisemitic slur adopted by white supremacists. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories TV 'My Favorite Murder' HBO Series Sets December Premiere TV Melissa Gorga to Debut 'Sunday Sauce' Video Podcast This December (Exclusive)

Yang used the pejorative term to describe Spektor, a Jewish singer and artist, in his Oct. 14 podcast alongside co-host Matt Rogers and guest Edy Monica. The popular podcast produced by Will Ferrell &#x2019;s Big Money Players Podcast Network and iHeartRadio also streams on YouTube.

Meghan McCain was among those to speak out against Yang for his comment, writing on social media , &#x201C;Bowen Yang using antisemitic slang that was popularized and mainstreamed by KKK leader David Duke is really just chefs kiss for the guy who got Shane Gillis fired from SNL for racism.&#x201C;

For their Las Culturistas podcast, Yang and Rogers give their takes on pop culture moments with celebrity guests and recurring segments. The show launched in 2016 and has featured guests including Mariah Carey, Michelle Obama, Lady Gaga, Reese Witherspoon, Ariana Grande, Charli XCX, Chappell Roan and Nicole Kidman. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

The podcast has become known for its &#x201C;I Don&#x2019;t Think So, Honey&#x201D; segment in which the celebrities join Yang and Rogers in ranting about something in modern culture for one minute as well as the question asked to every guest: &#x201C;What is the culture that made you say culture was for you?&#x201D;

